add(1515,'boundary','Exact Nyquist boundary','For x(t) = sin(2π·8000t) sampled at exactly 16000 Hz with n = 0, 1, 2, …, what does the sampled sequence show about an unqualified “at least twice” rule?',[
('All samples are zero, so this boundary/phase case loses the sinusoid despite equality of the rates.','sin(πn) vanishes for integer n.'),('The samples alternate between +1 and −1, so every phase is recovered identically.','That alternating sequence belongs to a cosine phase, not this sine.'),('Every sample is +1, so only the amplitude is preserved.','The displayed sine evaluates to zero at the sample times.'),('The samples contain a 16 kHz sinusoid distinct from zero under discrete-time observation.','A sampled sequence cannot distinguish this zero sequence from no signal.')],'At the exact Nyquist boundary, phase matters. The usual safe reconstruction statement uses a bandlimited spectrum strictly below half the sample rate.',typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['t_n = n/16000.','x[n] = sin(2π·8000n/16000) = sin(πn) = 0.'],conflict='The slide states an unqualified at-least-twice reconstruction slogan without the boundary/phase and bandlimiting qualifications.',locator='Nyquist theorem bullet.',assumptions=['Added pure sine, specified phase and sample origin; ideal exact sampling.'])
